  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Mercantour is one of the natural jewels of the Southern Alps — but it's also a national park, with one rule that has no exceptions: dogs are strictly banned from the park's core zone, even on a leash or carried in your arms. This ban, common to every French national park, has been in place since the park was created in 1979 (source: Mercantour National Park).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Only livestock protection, herding or guarding dogs, guide or assistance dogs and, under conditions, dogs of residents of certain hamlets are allowed in the core zone. The park invites everyone else to choose a hike outside the core, in the buffer zone (aire d'adhésion), where the park has no environmental regulation of its own (communes' local rules still need checking; keep your dog on a leash if there are flocks). Before you set off, check carefully whether your chosen route crosses the core zone or stays in the buffer zone — a map of the zones is available on the national park's website.</p>
      <p>In the buffer zone, particularly in the Haut-Verdon sector (Allos, Villars-Colmars, Colmars-les-Alpes), a flock-tracking tool called Pastorando lets you spot the position of flocks — and therefore the guard dogs with them — almost in real time, via lead ewes fitted with GPS collars. Launched in 2022 in Allos and then extended to Villars-Colmars and the Lignins, it only covers these Haut-Verdon sectors, not the whole Mercantour.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: head for the valleys and trails in the buffer zone — just as spectacular, and far more numerous than the core zone, which covers only a tiny fraction of French territory.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The resorts and villages around the Mercantour (the Tinée and Vésubie valleys, Ubaye) offer plenty of hike starting points in the buffer zone, to explore with your dog on a leash, making sure the route stays outside the park's core zone.</p>
